{% extends "_layouts/work.nunjucks" %}
{% import "_modules/molecules/buttonslider/buttonslider.nunjucks" as buttonslider %}
{% block config %}
    {% set baseUrl = "../" %}
{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
    <article class="section section-top-padding case-study" id="fashion_rebrand_container">
        <section class="gallery-container">
            <div class="black-bg">
                <img src="../images/work/thumbs/gallery_thumb_fashion.jpg" title="Fashion Rebrand" width="100%" height="100%" style="margin-bottom:-12px">
            </div>
            <div class="container">
                <div class="document-text text-box">

                    <!-- 01 HERO -->
                    <header class="cs-hero">
                        <p class="cs-eyebrow">Case Study — Fashion Rebrand</p>
                        <h1>Relaunching a Fashion Brand Experience on Web</h1>
                        <p class="cs-subtitle">
                            Redesigning the UI visual identity to uplift our products — while improving Landing Page sessions and reducing exit rates for a global fashion retailer.
                        </p>
                        <p class="cs-byline">H&amp;M ⋅ Staff Designer ⋅ Web Global Redesign ⋅ 9 months</p>

                        <div class="cs-metrics">
                            <div class="cs-metric">
                                <span class="cs-metric-value" data-countup="20">20</span><span class="cs-metric-suffix">k</span>
                                <span class="cs-metric-label">new users per month*</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="cs-metric">
                                <span class="cs-metric-value" data-countup="14">14</span><span class="cs-metric-suffix">%</span>
                                <span class="cs-metric-label">single-page sessions <i class="ph ph-arrow-down"></i>**</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="cs-metric">
                                <span class="cs-metric-value" data-countup="27">27</span><span class="cs-metric-suffix">%</span>
                                <span class="cs-metric-label">new sessions <i class="ph ph-arrow-up"></i>**</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <p class="cs-footnote">* for two pilot markets &nbsp;·&nbsp; ** vs last year, May 3–31, across Landing &amp; Department pages</p>
                        <p class="cs-summary"><em>More sessions, fewer single-page sessions and fewer exits — up to ~20k more customers per month continued down the funnel toward transaction.</em></p>
                    </header>

                    <!-- 02 CONTEXT -->
                    <section class="cs-section" id="context">
                        <h2>Context</h2>
                        <p class="cs-section-intro">A global fashion retailer, losing its fashion credibility — and a nine-month rebrand mandate covering the digital and physical experience.</p>

                        <blockquote class="cs-pullquote">
                            Brand recognition, sales and customer retention are dropping. We are no longer considered a fashion brand — we are considered a retail brand.
                        </blockquote>

                        <div class="cs-cards">
                            <div class="cs-card">
                                <h3>Vision &amp; Mission</h3>
                                <p><strong>Vision:</strong> re-establish ourselves as a fashion brand with a focus on quality.</p>
                                <p><strong>Mission:</strong> rebrand the digital and physical experience to regain our fashion credibility.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="cs-card">
                                <h3>The Customer</h3>
                                <p>The influential <em>Fashionista</em> — 18–34, fashion-ambitious, knows what they like, open to expanding their style, wants to share and be shared with. A <em>Validator</em>, not a Guidance Seeker.</p>
                                <p class="cs-note">Hypothesis: bring in the Fashionista, and the regular customer — influenced by these trendsetters — follows and invests more in quality, stylish apparel.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="cs-card">
                                <h3>Core Team</h3>
                                <p>A senior cross-functional team of 15: 3 brand designers, 3 UX designers, 2 UI designers, 2 design system designers, 2 researchers and 3 project managers.</p>
                                <p>I was Lead Web UX of the global rebrand, responsible for ensuring the brand lift met the standards for Web.</p>
                                <p class="cs-note">⚠️ Looking back, we were too centralised and waterfall — not enough room for contribution from teams and developers.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <h3>Goals</h3>
                        <ul class="cs-facts">
                            <li>Lift the fashion &amp; brand on App &amp; Web.</li>
                            <li>Unify the experience through Branding, Content, UI and UX.</li>
                            <li>Declutter and focus.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </section>

                    <!-- 03 PROCESS -->
                    <section class="cs-section" id="process">
                        <h2>Process</h2>
                        <p class="cs-section-intro">
                            A repeating Discovery → Exploration → Delivery loop: gather insights from behavioural metrics and formative studies, prioritise problems and opportunities, ideate and validate through summative studies, then ship with developers — and feed the learnings back in.
                        </p>
                        <ol class="cs-steps">
                            <li class="cs-step">
                                <span class="cs-number">01</span>
                                <div>
                                    <h3>Strategy</h3>
                                    <p>Proposed a cyclical workflow: the Core Team co-creates the concepts for each core view and pattern, which teams then use as their reference.</p>
                                </div>
                            </li>
                            <li class="cs-step">
                                <span class="cs-number">02</span>
                                <div>
                                    <h3>Exploration</h3>
                                    <p>Mapped insights to the customer journey, workshopped the themes, and ran desirability studies on the top-3 concepts.</p>
                                </div>
                            </li>
                            <li class="cs-step">
                                <span class="cs-number">03</span>
                                <div>
                                    <h3>Guidance</h3>
                                    <p>Built the Web &amp; Visual Guidelines — breakpoint and layout specs, design standards and Design Reviews across teams, with SEO and Accessibility forums to align them.</p>
                                </div>
                            </li>
                            <li class="cs-step">
                                <span class="cs-number">04</span>
                                <div>
                                    <h3>QA</h3>
                                    <p>Established the Design QA initiative across dozens of teams — ~50 visual issues documented in 2 weeks.</p>
                                </div>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </section>

                    <!-- 04 DEEP-DIVE: ENTRANCES -->
                    <section class="cs-section" id="deep-dive">
                        <h2>Deep-Dive: Entrances</h2>

                        <div class="cs-cards">
                            <div class="cs-card">
                                <h3>Problem</h3>
                                <p>Customers exit early from Department pages — a fragmented experience with limited discovery. The Primary Navigation always starts at the highest level of department views, which is redundant when the customer wants a context-sensitive list one level down. The old Desktop page exposed a lot of navigation — good for ease of access, but overwhelming for the new customers who mostly use Web. And a landing page that looks unfocused, with unclear navigation, makes people bounce.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="cs-card">
                                <h3>Goal &amp; Hypothesis</h3>
                                <p><strong>Goal:</strong> increase traffic down the funnel, let departments be discovered, and set them as the default landing page for returning customers.</p>
                                <p><strong>Hypothesis:</strong> make the Index Page the Women's department with the other departments exposed as tabs — focusing the start page while making each department easier to find and segment on.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <h3>Simplifying the Information Architecture</h3>
                        <p>
                            The Start Page became the Women's Department, and Kids &amp; Babies were merged into Kids. A small change with a big impact on the customer landing experience, our ways of working, and SEO. The research foundation came from our CX Unit and Core Researchers; from here, summative studies validated the proposals.
                        </p>

                        <div class="cs-comparison">
                            <div class="cs-panel">
                                <span class="cs-tag">Before</span>
                                <h3>Start Page</h3>
                                <p>A cluttered index with heavy, exposed navigation competing for attention — unfocused for new customers.</p>
                                <div class="cs-frame">
                                    <span class="cs-frame-label"><strong>Start Page — Before</strong>Final mockup pending</span>
                                </div>
                                <div class="cs-annotation">
                                    <span class="cs-annotation-dot"></span>
                                    <p>Overwhelming navigation options</p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="cs-panel">
                                <span class="cs-tag">After</span>
                                <h3>Start Page</h3>
                                <p>The Women's department as the index — Women · Men · Kids · Home · Beauty as tabs — with bigger, bolder typography, less copy, adjusted spacing and media in focus.</p>
                                <div class="cs-frame">
                                    <span class="cs-frame-label"><strong>Start Page — After</strong>Final mockup pending</span>
                                </div>
                                <div class="cs-annotation">
                                    <span class="cs-annotation-dot"></span>
                                    <p>Simplified tab navigation</p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="cs-wide">
                            <figure class="cs-showcase">
                                <div class="cs-frame cs-frame--tall">
                                    <span class="cs-frame-label"><strong>The new Start Page — Women's department as the index</strong>Big page-wide mockup · final image pending</span>
                                </div>
                                <figcaption>Bigger, bolder typography with less copy, media in focus — and Women · Men · Kids · Home · Beauty exposed as tabs.</figcaption>
                            </figure>
                        </div>

                        <div class="cs-comparison">
                            <div class="cs-panel">
                                <span class="cs-tag">Before</span>
                                <h3>Navigation</h3>
                                <p>Desktop hover mega-menu: easy to jump anywhere from anywhere, but a lot of information and multiple patterns to keep track of across Mobile &amp; Desktop. App and Web menus lived in completely different places.</p>
                                <div class="cs-frame">
                                    <span class="cs-frame-label"><strong>Navigation — Before</strong>Final mockup pending</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="cs-panel">
                                <span class="cs-tag">After</span>
                                <h3>Navigation</h3>
                                <p>Progressive disclosure — navigation hidden behind a hamburger/icons, unified across desktop and mobile. On mobile, horizontal department tabs anchored to the header: hidden on scroll down, revealed on scroll up.</p>
                                <div class="cs-frame">
                                    <span class="cs-frame-label"><strong>Navigation — After</strong>Final mockup pending</span>
                                </div>
                                <div class="cs-annotation">
                                    <span class="cs-annotation-dot"></span>
                                    <p>Mobile-friendly progressive disclosure</p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="cs-wide">
                            <figure class="cs-showcase">
                                <div class="cs-frame cs-frame--tall">
                                    <span class="cs-frame-label"><strong>The new Primary Navigation — Department tabs</strong>Big page-wide mockup · final image pending</span>
                                </div>
                                <figcaption>Departments always accessible in the header — hidden on scroll down, revealed on scroll up, with the active department shown at all times.</figcaption>
                            </figure>
                        </div>

                        <h3>Key insights that shaped the design</h3>
                        <ul class="cs-facts">
                            <li>Customers overwhelmingly get inspired on social media before entering our store.</li>
                            <li>Women often navigated to Men's departments anyway — shopping, or at least browsing, for their partners.</li>
                            <li>Customers switch between browsing and seeking modes within a single session.</li>
                            <li>Web users are often new and unfamiliar with us; App is used by Members who know the brand.</li>
                            <li>Mobile is ~65% of web traffic, but Desktop still often carries the transactions.</li>
                        </ul>

                        <h3>Validation — Qualitative Discoverability Study</h3>
                        <p>
                            The Navigation Team and I created the research plan with our Core Team Researchers, who ran the study and reported back. <strong>Participants:</strong> 9 (1 male, 8 female), ages 21–37, DE/UK/US, regular shoppers. <strong>Method:</strong> remote moderated, old and new versions of the start page across App, Mobile and Desktop prototypes.
                        </p>
                        <ul class="cs-facts">
                            <li>Department navigation was a very intuitive way to move between departments.</li>
                            <li>The exposed department tab was validated as worth pursuing.</li>
                            <li>Departments exposed upfront on Mobile would help new and returning customers explore.</li>
                            <li>Web and App need fundamentally different navigation menus — the bar and expectations differ per device.</li>
                            <li>Fewer departments reduce complexity and increase the presence of existing ones.</li>
                            <li>Key issue: some customers may miss the Desktop mega-menu — one participant preferred the hover-over effect.</li>
                        </ul>

                        <h3>Delivery — two teams, one experience</h3>
                        <p>
                            The experience was owned by two teams in two verticals: the Entrances Team (mine) for Landing and Department pages, and the Navigation Team for the Navigation Menu. To the customer it's one experience — so we worked as one. I helped build the Web navigation menus with the Navigation Designer, to ensure the connecting tissue.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            The Department Navigation Tab became a single shared component, used the same way in the header and the nav menu, on Mobile and Desktop:
                        </p>
                        <ul class="cs-facts">
                            <li>Always in relation to the Header on Mobile &amp; Desktop — a familiar placement.</li>
                            <li>Displays the active Department at all times in Navigation.</li>
                            <li>Horizontal swipe on text overflow on Mobile; visible on page load.</li>
                            <li>Hidden on scroll down, revealed on scroll up — out of the way, but always accessible.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p class="cs-note">
                            Implementation realities: font tokens felt disproportionately small on Desktop at release; an oversized menu came from right-hand tiles being too small; Kids needed an extra age level, so its layout differed; Women/Men could be set automatically from the gender chosen at account creation. Navigation icons without supportive text are a usability risk — but these were tested long before, and familiar enough to justify on Desktop.
                        </p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- 05 OUTCOMES -->
                    <section class="cs-section" id="outcomes">
                        <h2>Outcomes</h2>
                        <p class="cs-section-intro">Pilot results, May 3–31 year over year, two markets — early, but very promising.</p>

                        <div class="cs-wide">
                        <table class="cs-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Metric</th>
                                    <th>Before</th>
                                    <th>After</th>
                                    <th>What it means</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Sessions</td>
                                    <td>~330k / mo</td>
                                    <td>~450k / mo</td>
                                    <td>+27% — such a shift YoY is often more than season &amp; marketing</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Single-page sessions</td>
                                    <td>~10%</td>
                                    <td>~8.5%</td>
                                    <td>−14% — ~11k more sessions going beyond the first page</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Exit rate</td>
                                    <td>~15%</td>
                                    <td>~13%</td>
                                    <td>−11% — ~9k fewer sessions exiting in a month</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Performance</td>
                                    <td>Baseline</td>
                                    <td>+40%</td>
                                    <td>Faster pages improved the experience and SEO ranking</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        </div>

                        <ul class="cs-facts">
                            <li>All departments gained traffic from their new presence on Mobile Web and Apps.</li>
                            <li>Business Units were happy with better discoverability, content guidelines and UI elements to work with.</li>
                            <li>Cross-area collaboration with Brand and Content improved — campaign production time &amp; cost halved.</li>
                            <li>Web experience and SEO improved thanks to consolidating department pages and reducing loading times, largely from less content on the Index and Department pages.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p class="cs-note">⚠️ Rebrands are often a long-term investment — we could have done more quantification work and gotten more solid insights.</p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- 06 FUTURE IMPROVEMENTS -->
                    <section class="cs-section" id="future">
                        <h2>Future Improvements, Based on Learnings</h2>
                        <ul class="cs-facts">
                            <li>Department Navigation on other parts of the journey.</li>
                            <li>Using Department preferences to shape recommendations.</li>
                            <li>Expanding the Department Tab pattern to be used elsewhere, like Categories.</li>
                            <li>Mobile is ~65% of web traffic while Desktop often carries transactions — a signal for further Mobile Web improvements.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </section>

                    <!-- 07 LESSONS & RELEVANCE -->
                    <section class="cs-section" id="lessons">
                        <h2>Lessons &amp; Relevance</h2>
                        <div class="cs-twocol">
                            <div class="cs-col">
                                <h3>Lessons learned</h3>
                                <ul>
                                    <li><strong>Big projects are very risky</strong> — learn early and often to de-risk.</li>
                                    <li><strong>Include teams earlier in the process</strong> — committee-based design at this scale is harmful for culture and high risk.</li>
                                    <li><strong>Up-front navigation helps discovery</strong> — users find what they're looking for more easily when it's visible.</li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>
                            <div class="cs-col">
                                <h3>Relevance as a Staff Designer</h3>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>How to tackle big cross-departmental navigation challenges — in process and team coordination.</li>
                                    <li>How Brand can be better integrated into product.</li>
                                    <li>How to bring teams together on a greater, unified mission.</li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </section>
                </div>
            </div>
            {{buttonslider.content({
            nexthref: site.data.project_fashion_rebrand.nexthref,
            prevhref: site.data.project_fashion_rebrand.prevhref,
            text: var.linkname
            })}}
        </section>
    </article>
{% endblock %}
